A||N||37||87V6GT||Front Licence Plate Problems with the Law||||11||
Z||000000||87V6GT||08-10-2001||07:47 PM||||My son and his family have been experiencing some police harrasment lately, due to not wanting to put the front plate on any of their aero nose Fieros. To "try" and make a long story a bit short, only his 16 year old son has actually ever been pulled over, in my sons (his Dads) 86 Fiero no less. His son has an 85GT with an unusual paint scheme. It's a light yellow green with purple trim. My son drives his red 86 that also has an aero nose. My son (Fiero5) has NEVER been pulled over for not having a front plate, and neither have I. I drive pretty regularly my maroon 87 GT, also without the front plate. My son at first chalked it up to a young kid in a flashy sports car, and figured it would die down after a bit.<BR>His son Stephen has driven his 85 GT daily and has been pulled over many times for not displaying the front plate properly (his son decided to go plateless on the nose like I have and his Dad), but luckily had only received a warning all those times. His father (My son Steve) drives his red 86 daily and has never been pulled over. Steve has driven past the local Police a few times when they have been sittying beside the road watching traffic in his red 86, with no front plate, and has even turned around and gone back by the police a second and third time just to see if they would pull him over. Then one day my son Steve was working on his sons 85GT when his son had to go to work. So he said to take his red 86. His son got pulled over in the 86 and this time got a ticket ($72.)<BR>My son called the cheif of Police up and filed a complainyt saying that this was harrasment, since he had never been pulled over by them the many, many times he had driven by them.<BR>My son looked into the NH law about the front plate, and discovered a very vague law. It is not specific and even the people at the DMV in Concord have stated that the law is open to enterpretation. They said one officer could agree with us and another may not. We do not want to put our plates on the aero nose for three reasons. 1) it makes the Fiero look ugly 2) it causes the flow of air to be obstructed going thru the aero opening 3) it has been said to cause engine heating problems and so forth to the air obstruction<P>What do other aero nose Fiero owners think about the front plate?<P>What would be nice, is some testimonials of any kind about what effect, if any, you have experienced with having a front plate mounted in the "correct" place on the aero nose. Being that the aero plate bracket is now discontinued by GM, this would be a good time to push fior some sort of legislation allowing either no front plates in NH at all, or at least a limted exemption for certian cars like the aero nose Fieros, some Vettes and so on. The street rods have an exemption for no front plate in NH, so it isn't unheard of in this state. There are also other states in the country that have no front plate law. How is that working out so far. Ray Paulk, President of NEFA-New England Fiero Association) says that one complaint about the Fieros not having a front plate is that the Police radar has nothing solid to hit. He said Mass went back to two plates also because robbers and such could back away from a crime as no one could then ID the plates.<P>Let us know what you think on this subject and any experiences either with the Police or with the air restrictions due to the plate over the front ait intake.<P>Russ Kelley<BR>87 GT (maroon)<BR>87 GT (Blue)<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Merrimack-RussKelley3rd.jpg"> ||||reg||11
Z||000001||theogre||08-10-2001||08:05 PM||||for the legal problem... Your only real option is to contact state regulators and push them to pass clear rules or maybe they can make DMV clarify it. Don't know how NH runs... That or wait untill you get a ticket for it and run it thru state courts.<P>Could a tag mess with air flow? Yes. Does it on Fiero? maybe. Need a wind tunnel to really tell if and how bad. Easy test is mount one up and see if the temp is higher than normal.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||08-10-2001||08:07 PM||||Russ, It was told to me like this;<BR>If you go to the DMV to reg. or get plates for your car, they give you TWO (2) plates one front one rear?? if that is the case then you must have TWO plates on your car.<P>The DMV doesn't ask  "How many plates would you like" The law says 2 plates.<BR>Now that is just the way it was told to me??<P>DXR_DAD<P>DXR_DAD||||reg||4
Z||000003||Old Lar||08-10-2001||08:13 PM||||New York requires front flates and I have them on both my GT with the aero nose.  I don't get harassed by the police and I don't have over heating problems with either cars.  I suspect that if you are having overheating problems, there is a problem with the cooling system, not caused by a licence plate.<P>My 88 at Williamsburg. Front plate in place.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/MyGTM_1.jpg"> <P>MY 87 with front plate in place.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Backyard_1.jpg"> <P>When you know what the rules are and you intentionally violate them and get caught, I shed no tears.  If the law is incorrect, or needs to be changed, then work at changing it.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||87V6GT||08-10-2001||08:14 PM||||They have gotten a ticket, at least my grandson has. The problem is that he is 17 and doesnt feel luike being harrased and worrying every time he drives by a police car if they are going to stop him. He has decided to put the plate on the nose and get the ticket dropped. If this was his Dad (my son), he would fight it as far as he could (the supreme court if needed). But as I said, they won't pull over my son who is ready to have it out with the Police, as much as he's tried by going by them on purpose many times. He can't fight the battle if his son doesn't want to pursue it under the ticket his son got.<P>Russ Kelley||||reg||9
Z||000005||87V6GT||08-10-2001||08:41 PM||||OK, here is the law out of a copy we obtained:<P>Saf-C 3212.03 Registartion Plates<BR>(a) The registration plates issued by the department shall be mounted securely on the exterior of the vehicle in the location provided by the manufacturer.<BR>(b) When 2 registration plates are issued, one shall be mounted on the front of the vehicle and the second shall be mounted on the rear.<BR>(c) If no location has been provided by the manufacturer, the plates shall be securely mounted with a bracket or comparable method in a visible location, and the rear plate shall be illuminated by a white license plate light when the headlights or parking lights are on.<BR>(d) All license plates shall be mounted right side up.<P>------------------<P>As you can see, if we agree that on the Indy nose/GT (aero) the original design did not take into effect the creation of a plate mount, this would then have the aero nose style Fieros fall under (c). Notice that (a) states specifically external, but if we follow (c) it doesn't say external or in the front for that matter. I know it is being picky, but so be it. Steve and his son had mounted the front plates under the windshield on the bottom right hand side using small pieces of velcro. This didn't obscure the view out of the window either as the Fiero windshield is so large and the plates sits far enough down at that point it works great. It is viewable from the front of the vehicle and stays within the law "as written" as you can see. However, since Steve can't get them to push him on it, they only want to pick on his kid. Steve's name has been getting around Concord's DMV though as when he called later after stirring it up a bit with the local and State Police trying to get specific info on the plate mounting law, he then called the State DMV in Concord looking for clarifcation. Everyone he talked to recognized his name at that point. "Oh yeah, you are the guy that refuses to put your front plate on your car correctly!" The Stae Police person who handles this type of thing even told Steve that he was going to ask for a hearing asking that Steve's right to register cars be taken away. He said he will take all "his" paperwork to the hearing and have the book thrown at Steve. Steve did discover during the phone conversation with this guy, that the plate bracket number that he is going to take into the hearing and tell everyone is readily available and help counter Steve's argument is the wrong plate bracket, one for a coupe nose. The guy even was beligerent to Steve in the converstation, and Steve amazingly kept his cool and stayed calm and stayed with facts, that obviously upset this guy to no end.<P>The local Police Chief has had a few conversations with Steve at Steve's request, and the Chief has since said he decided at this point his dept. is going to leave the Keley Fieros alone unless he is doing 100 mph or something, and waite to see what the DMV decides if anything. It has been a few months now, and we have heard nothing further from them. But now that Steve and his kids name has gotten around the State, it could be taking on a life of it's own.<BR>Big Brother is watching.<P>Russ Kelley||||reg||12
Z||000006||Old Lar||08-10-2001||08:47 PM||||There are lots of laws that are  "unreasonable"  There is an ordinance in one of the local communities that say if they (the sound police) can hear your car radio at 25 feet from the car it is a court appearence.  It also is a $150 dollar fine.  Twice and the car gets towed and a $300 fine. Then the cost of the tow and impound fee. Fair?  But when I'm in my house and can hear the boombox cars a quarter mile away, its well worth it for the law.  If it were enforced consistantly.<P>If you want to hire lawyers and fight the laws that would be $150 an hour for your lawyer and your "lost" wages, vacation time, for appearences in court, go for it.  I think that most states or localities can out spend you with your efforts to "fight" in court.<P>The Fiero Store sells the Front plate bracket for the aero nose p/n 67303 for $21.95.<p>[This message has been edited by Old Lar (edited 08-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||btoth||08-10-2001||09:40 PM||||I would think that if an AMERICAN auto manufacturer doesn't design a place to mount the plate without having to purchase a dorky plastic bracket then you shouldn't have to put the front plate on.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Of course, under this rule... all Corvette C5 owners would have to remove the cool cover on the front and put a plate there instead.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Wouldn't that look dorky!||||reg||1
Z||000008||isthiswhereiputausername?||08-11-2001||12:20 AM||||I was 18 when I first got my fiero, they LOVED stopping me. <P>I even had a cop apologize here to me before admitting he acted in an unprofessional manner during a traffic stop.<BR>(I had the conversation taped on a minicasette player-best purchase I made)<P><BR>Advice?   Tell your grandson to watch what he says if he gets stopped.. and get the officers names/badge-ids and time  of the incident, and if he gets stopped numerous times for bogus reasons, he can bring this info in and plead harrassment.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||cadaver||08-11-2001||12:47 AM||||Im over it. Either put it on or risk the tickets becuase you know its illegal. The fact that you cant get a bracket easily is of no concern. I have an 86GT with no front plate and dont plan on putting one on. And I will have to deal with it if the time comes.||||reg||1
Z||000010||artherd||08-11-2001||02:14 AM||||I'm in CA, and have no front plates.<P>There is no provision on my car for mounting one, and I am concerned about making an already dangerously lifty nose worse. (coupe nose)<P>If they want to ticket me, fine, I'll fight it.<P>I WILL NOT endanger my life.<P>They can all bugger off.<P>AFAIK in CA is is perfectly legal, even though they issue two plates.  At least, it's never been an issue.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000011||Sleeper||08-11-2001||09:52 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B><BR>AFAIK in CA is is perfectly legal, even though they issue two plates.  At least, it's never been an issue.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>i'll have to dispute that cuz i live in CA too and the cops used to pull me over just because i didn't have a front plate. its the law in CA to have both plates on your car. i put it in the windshield and they have not bugged me since about it but i know if i get some dick cop he'll give me a ticket and tell me to put on the front of the car.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Stage-2GT||08-11-2001||11:08 AM||||  A seventeen year old kid will get pulled over for sneezing wrong. All those stops were probably just to check him out and the plate issue was jus PC, probable cause. <BR>  How many of the C4 and C5 vettes do you see around with a front plate? NONE!!! You think that a Vette owner is going to damage the front of his $40,000+  fascia for a plate? Hell no! If you did a survey, you would find that the people that get pulled over like that are generally of mid to low income and or, driving an associate class of vehical. Now last I checked the Fiero wasn't in the upper class. <BR>  I agree, it's harrasment, plain and simple. I have no idea how to fight it other than asking the judge, how many Vette or Porcshe owners he sees for this type of ticket.<BR>  Let's put it like it is. If you had a middle aged black male, a middle aged white male and a teen aged hot chick all driving down the same road and each one performed an illeagle manuver that warrented a stop, who would get the ticket? Oh, and put that in descending order from first to last. <BR>1.) the black male gets a ticket for a burnned out brake light.<BR>2.) the white male gets a ticket for exhibition of speed in a 25MPH zone.<BR>3.) the teen aged hot chick gets a warning for failure to yeild, no turn signal, making an illeagle U-Turn and attempted evasion.<BR> You may think I'm joking, but I'm not. Now you can substitute a white teen aged male for the black male and you have a very typical scenario.<BR>  I was witness to a DUI accident two years ago. The woman driver of a van that rearended a new dodge truck, was PLASTERED!! She couldn't even begin to pass the road side test. As I was giving my statement to officer #2, I heard officer #1 ask the lady if her husband was home. She replyed yes. Officer #1 then said. "Let's give him a call so he can pick you up, otherwise I have to take you in. In fact, I should be taking you anyway because you were given a warning by my partner less than an hour ago".<BR>  Iasked O #2 If that was leagle, he told me to mind my own business. I got a little upset and said a few things. Next thing I know, I, the witness, was cuffed in the back of the car where I finished my interview. After the lady and her husband left, I was let out of the car and given a field sobriety test. When I made my complaint I was told that the officers must have had due cause for detention and testing. <BR> Ok, back on track. Your grandson will have to deal with that kinda crap unless he's riding a bycicle. But make sure that the bycicle isn't to flashy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>S2GT  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><BR>  <P>------------------<BR>"If Hate Was Money, I Would Own The World"<p>[This message has been edited by Stage-2GT (edited 08-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||fierobaby||08-11-2001||11:12 AM||||Here in Ohio we are required to have Front and BAck plates... but the law says they have to be in a visable area... why don't you put the front plate on the dash of the car? thats what we do out here and as long as you can read it its fine||||reg||1
Z||000014||Fierowrecker||08-11-2001||03:32 PM||||Hey Russ!<BR>I simpathsize, but if they give you two plates, then you need to display two plates...<BR>HOWEVER...<BR>I would display the plate through the front window, or UNDER the aero nose, back 6 or 8 inches where it is hard to see, but it IS there!<BR>OR you could move to Michigan, we only have one plate...<BR>crash...<p>[This message has been edited by Fierowrecker (edited 08-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||pontiacs4me||08-11-2001||04:07 PM||||I love kansas.  Only one plate.  If we were required to have two plates, I would mount my front plate under my aero nose, so it would rub and spark and scrape itself down to nothing, and I would have like 1 inch of it mounted under there, but guess what, its still there isnt it?  the cops can kiss my ass.||||reg||1
Z||000016||starfighter007@msn.com||08-11-2001||04:15 PM||||I've read what you posted about the law .And I dont see anything vague in there.I am guessing that the only time that you would be issued one plate would be for a trailer,Or motorcycle other wise you must display front and rear plate.||||reg||1
Z||000017||cadaver||08-11-2001||05:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B><BR>There is no provision on my car for mounting one, and I am concerned about making an already dangerously lifty nose worse. (coupe nose)<BR>I WILL NOT endanger my life.<P>They can all bugger off.<P>AFAIK in CA is is perfectly legal, even though they issue two plates.  At least, it's never been an issue.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Endanger your life by adding the front plate to a fiero. Ok you lost me. You think the plate will give the car too much lift. How fast do you drive. Over 200. Wow I have got it see this car.<P>And I too live in California and yes its illegal to only have one.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pontiacs4me:<BR><B>I love kansas.  Only one plate.  If we were required to have two plates, I would mount my front plate under my aero nose, so it would rub and spark and scrape itself down to nothing, and I would have like 1 inch of it mounted under there, but guess what, its still there isnt it?  the cops can kiss my ass.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I would live in Kansas for anything.<BR>And NO if it was ground off it wouldn't still be there would it? Especially of something the owner did to change the stock location. Im sure you will also tell the cop that pulls you over that he can kiss your ass while your bent over showing him your plate.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||rogergarrison||08-11-2001||06:45 PM||||Mount it on a length of piano hinge, under the front of bumper. stopped its there visible, driving the wind will blow it back. and when its parked at a show youd have to get on your knees to see it. As I read your law if there is no factory bracket, it just has to be secured someway and be visible from the front. If you dont want to try the hinge idea just make a few 'L' brackets UNDER the bumper just leaving enough room to clear the ground. Wont hurt anything if it scrapes a little going into a drive. I hate them myself, but what ya gonna do I had to put one on my front too, and Ferraris dont have any bracket either.<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000019||DXR_DAD||08-11-2001||07:34 PM||||I'm in Cali. also and you do need to have both plates, Front and rear that's the law<P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||4
Z||000020||mrgone||08-11-2001||08:47 PM||||get some suction cups and put it in the windshield||||reg||1
Z||000021||AusFiero||08-11-2001||09:18 PM||||    In all Australian states we have to run a front plate. Why? Because it is metal and thats the one the police radar cameras take a photo of when speeding. Don't know what radar they are using but it seems Fieros are stealth agaisnt their system. Have sped past 3 radar traps at over 20 KMH over te limit and have not got a ticket yet. <BR>    I got away with not running a front plate for 18 months but then the local law got into me 3 times in a week. So I put it on. Bolted straight into the front fasica on rubber mounts as the dorky looking original mount did not suit our local slimline plates.<BR>    I did ask the local police though why can a Lamborghini or an E type Jag not have them yet I have too even though it interferes with air flow. No answer, of course. I am not rich as the owners of them.<BR>    If anyone wants a dorky original GM one  have one I would sell. Just email me. ||||reg||1
Z||000022||87GTBro||08-11-2001||11:13 PM||||Here in Texas I have heard that it is the law to have both plates on your car.  However, I have been driving for seven YEARS and none of my cars have ever had a front liscense plate and I have never been pulled over because of it.<P>I don't have one on my GT and I will NEVER have one on my GT.  I heard a rumor they are going to start enforcing the front plates because our law enforcement agencies will soon be outfitted with radar cameras which take pictures of the car and send you tickets in the mail.<P>If they ever do make me put in on, I will sand the plate down and repaint it in non-reflective flat base paint and mount it on the front dash.<P>Its not like there are a lot of GT Fieros in my town.  It is a city of almost 50,000 and there are three GT Fieros...  Two are mine, the other is my brothers!<P>------------------<BR>AOL IM: GTFiero86Dan<BR>'86 GT 4 speed<BR>'87 GT Auto<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87GTBrosig1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000023||JayUSA77||08-12-2001||01:43 AM||||i live in cali and i go racing quite frequently and therefore the cops are looking to pull you over for anything so this is what you do and listen carfully okay. Get 2 L brackets made out of metal and screw them in on the top of the license plate so that the metal is sticking away from the plate and keep the plate under your seat. When you see a cop get behind you you take it out and put it on the dash in the top left corner and if the cop says you didnt have one kindly say "sir are you sure its right here." after he is mind bogled he'll probably suggest to put it on the front of the car. AGREE with him and pay no attention and drive off. Worked for me SO FAR. NO GUARENTEES. <BR>J<P>------------------<BR>86' Black Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000024||fropuff||08-12-2001||02:00 AM||||I just got my new front fascia painted, and it looks so much better w/o a front plate.  I'm not going to put it on, even if I do get b*tched at.  "Officer, GM does not make these things anymore, and all your tickets are cleaning out my funds.  If you could buy me one, then I'd put it on.  Thanks."  I've got the one stashed behind the passenger seat.  I'll probably mount it on the windshield w/ suction cups sooner or later.  BTW: front plates do reflect most of the radar that the cops shoot at you.  With no front plate, they probably won't get any back.  I was going 72/73 in a 55, and my radar detector went off.  But the cop didn't get me.  And he's a bastard.  But I forgot that they have rear radars too.  Saw the brake lights; ducked into the gas station before he turned around.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>PS: Russ, I emailed you.  Thanks!  ||||reg||1
Z||000025||FieroPerformance||08-14-2001||01:54 AM||||I live in Ohio where there is a law about the front plate. Not only does it obstruct air flow the the radiator but it also ruins the paint where it mounts. I have been driving w/o the front plate for about 8 months and haven't had any trouble with the police. I've even been on the police hot list and been called in for passing on double yellow without the front plate being an issue. I got pulled over once for almost hitting a police car on a wet road and he let me off with no ticket or mention of the missing plate. I was 16 at the time so I think if they were going to pick on someone it would have been me.<P>-Charlie<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000026||chester||08-14-2001||11:38 AM||||I live in New Hampshire and get my fair share of harassment.  But this is all silly. They give you two tags, they expect two tags, USE TWO TAGS!  End of story, no more hassle, law not broken, period.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">  Lets face it, the Fiero draws enough attention.  The LAST thing you want to do is push the limits.  If you do push the limits then pay the price and don't complain.  I know this from experience.  You WON'T win.  It's a lose lose situation. I had the same thing happen to me years ago.  I had a Chevy eater license plate on my Buick.  Got stopped by a rookie with a training officer.  The rookie was going to let me go but the other D**K made him write me.  Guess what - I was in the wrong and got what was coming to me.  Oh, and there is no problem with air flow if you have the front plate.  Been on my car since the day it was born.  <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigline.jpg">    <BR>Modified 2.8<BR>2.5" Drop<BR>11" Brakes<BR>17" Revolutions<BR>RCC Coilover Suspension<p>[This message has been edited by chester (edited 08-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000027||Springtyme||08-14-2001||12:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrgone:<BR><B>get some suction cups and put it in the windshield</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>then the cops can pull you over cause you have something obstructing your view. Ya just can't win with the cops  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>            ~Springtyme~||||reg||14
Z||000028||BC-GT||08-14-2001||12:37 PM||||I don't think there is any question as to whether it affects airflow, it doesn't. It just a darn good reason to get that ugly looking sore of the front of our cars.<P>------------------<BR>Darryl Martin<BR>1985 GT <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.imagestation.com/picture/pf251a969c96964ef15691e97e7ac4489/feb64e3a.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000029||SlingBlade||08-14-2001||12:52 PM||||In Texas, we have more "Regulations" than laws. I have received legal counsel, and my brother obtained counsel independently, and both lawyers said that in Texas, the back tag must be on the car, however, the front tag only has to be in your "possession" if you get pulled over.  The DPS can use the missing plate as an excuse to pull you over. (Also, being a DPS regulation, a missing front plate is not a city cop's problem; unless there is a city ordinance for such and you live within that city's municipal corporate limits.)||||reg||1
Z||000030||Fiero5||08-14-2001||03:22 PM||||Quote from Newmarket, NH :          <BR>     "I live in New Hampshire and get my fair share of harassment. But this is all silly. They give you two tags, they expect two tags, USE TWO TAGS! End of story, no more hassle, law not broken, period."<P>There are exceptions to just about every law, including apparently this one in NH. Also, there are some states that do issue two plates, but only require that the rear one at least be mounted with working plate lights at night.<P>Fact: the registration plate law in NH "as written", does NOT say that the front plate has to be in a specific spot. If you call the DMV in Concord and talk to anyone, even as high up as the deputy director as I have, even the DD will reluctantly tell you that it is open to interpretation. This is ultimately the fight we will have if it happens. Who's interpritation is correct, if there even is a correct one with the current law as written.<BR>Therefore, I am NOT breaking the law if I choose to place it somewhere else, like say in the lower right hand portion of my windshield where it is not obstructing my view.<BR>Read the law again:<BR>"....the plates shall be securely mounted with a bracket or comparable method in a visible location,..."<BR>I have done that. My front plate is mounted in a viewable location. It is mounted in the front area of the car and is mounted securely.<BR>When I have pressed any law enforcement officer this way, I then get the interoretation crap.<P> We haven't lost, and probably won't in the long run. In the end, I will probably end up forcing the issue to the point where they will have to get the law re-written in more detail. We are also contacting our representative, Peter Burling. who just so happens to live a quarter mile down the road from us in Plainfield about getting a bill introduced to eliminate the front plate law. <P>My son only being 17 doesn't want to, nor should he have to or be expected to fight this however. I told him I would support whatever decision he made on it. He originally decided to go plateless like me and my Dad. At this point however, he has decided he doesn't want to feel scared everytime he drives by a cop, and so he now has a front plate which I carefully put on for him. However, I myself am willing to push it as far as needed. I didn't get up one morning and say I think I will piss off the cops today. They started it by harrassing my son by trying to enforce a law in a way that is not even written the way they have tried to enforce it. They just used it as an excuse to pull him over. Now, they will have to find some other reason to pull him over. <P>If we said we didn't want rear plate lights on our car or something like that, then I could see at that point that it's  just being dumb. But here they are interpreting the law incorrectly. I didn't write it and neither did the Police. If they don't like the way it is written, then they should go argue it out with those who wrote it that way.<P>I also disagree with some who have said that the air flow is not effected when the plate is mounted right in the middle of the aero nose opening. When mounted that way, it DOES effect the running of the Fiero, more on some and maybe less on others but it does effect it. As we know, Fiero engines can run a bit hot, especially in the hot summer air. To have the cooling air cut off by 75% and say that it doesn't effect the car is something I disagree with. If my plate is on the nose, the car runs hotter and the temp guage climbs quicker. This puts more stress on my engine. Without the plate mounted, it runs much cooler and the temp needle stays in the low normal area. That is a fact, not just my opinion. I have had one head crack which was expensive to replace, I do not plan on experiencing that again. Especially because a few idiot Police officers are bored and decide to harrass one of us by "interpreting" a certain law to fit their immediate need to be able to pull one of us over.<P>Steve||||reg||12
Z||000031||chester||08-15-2001||09:53 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero5:<BR><B>Quote from Newmarket, NH :          <BR>     "I live in New Hampshire and get my fair share of harassment. But this is all silly. They give you two tags, they expect two tags, USE TWO TAGS! End of story, no more hassle, law not broken, period."<P>Fact: the registration plate law in NH "as written", does NOT say that the front plate has to be in a specific spot. If you call the DMV in Concord and talk to anyone, even as high up as the deputy director as I have, even the DD will reluctantly tell you that it is open to interpretation. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Here is the law as your dad posted earlier;<P>Saf-C 3212.03 Registration Plates<BR>(a) The registration plates issued by the department shall be mounted securely on the exterior of the vehicle in the location provided by the manufacturer.<BR>(b) When 2 registration plates are issued, one shall be mounted on the front of the vehicle and the second shall be mounted on the rear.<BR>(c) If no location has been provided by the manufacturer, the plates shall be securely mounted with a bracket or comparable method in a visible location, and the rear plate shall be illuminated by a white license plate light when the headlights or parking lights are on.<BR>(d) All license plates shall be mounted right side up.<P>It's obvious that in order to meet the requirements of A the plates HAVE to be on the EXTERIOR of the car. Since there is no manufacture provided spot on your car then C goes into effect. I think that the amount of time and energy spent to litigate this is a waste of time. But then that is my opinion.  Kind of like the exhaust issue on Marian's car. I think you said she got stopped for a loud exhaust. Are you going to fight that legislation also?  I mean of course loud pipes save live right?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Just feel that putting the plate on the car is the obvious solution in this case.  You rebel!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigline.jpg">   <BR>Modified 2.8<BR>2.5" Drop<BR>11" Brakes<BR>17" Revolutions<BR>RCC Coilover Suspension||||reg||1
Z||000032||TylerB11||08-15-2001||11:32 AM||||1) GM still has the Brackets, I needed one for my 85GT, and they had it for me in a day.<P>2) I live in NJ, and they are the same with the 2 plate deal. I haven't noticed any cooling problems or anything.<P>3) Cops are dicks to teenagers. Many times they cross the line to harassment. Its a fact.<P>4) If you ever get a photo radar ticket in the states, throw it out and laugh. Repeat notice? repeat steps two and three (garbage and laugh). Photo radar has been declared unenforceable in the USA due to the fact that it interferes with your right to a fair trial. How?<BR>Scenario A:<BR>You get pulled over and ticketed for speeding. You fight it for whatever valid reason. You will remember every detail of that encounter for the trial.<P>Scenario B:<BR>You speed by photo radar. a month later you get a picture and a ticket in the mail. you think to yourself "when the hell was that?" and even if you do remember the situation, it affects your ability to defend yourself in court.||||reg||1
Z||000033||Indiana_resto_guy||08-15-2001||01:56 PM||||Were you issued 2 plates?<BR>The way it's written, if issued 2 plates the car (or whatever) is required to wear them both.<BR>There is also no noticeable effect on air to the radiator because of the front bracket on either the GT/SE nose or the '87 & '88 noses either.||||reg||1
Z||000034||Jaygee79||08-15-2001||03:32 PM||||I personally think it's a waste of time and money to fight it.  They gave you 2 plates, buy a bracket and put the front plate on.  Big deal.  90% of the country has to do it.<P>But that's just my opinion.  If you want to fight it, then fight it.||||reg||1
Z||000035||cadaver||08-17-2001||12:46 AM||||Im over it.||||reg||1
Z||000036||87V6GT||08-17-2001||08:00 PM||||starfighter007@msn.com wrote:<BR>"I've read what you posted about the law .And I dont see anything vague in there."<P>You didn't read it very well then.<BR>This part is where it is vague and leaves it open.<P>"(c) If no location has been provided by the manufacturer, the plates shall be securely mounted with a bracket OR COMPARABLE METHOD IN A VISIBLE LOCATION..."<P>Following this statement, you do NOT have to have it on the nose/front bumper.<P>Russ Kelley<P><BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Merrimack-RussKelley3rd.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000037||StuGood||08-18-2001||01:23 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pontiacs4me:<BR><B>I love Kansas.  Only one plate...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Yep, rear plates only in Kansas.  Kind of ironic, isn't it?  You'd think they'd give us three or four plates apiece, what with them being stamped right up there in Leavenworth  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>My 1986 SE had the OE front plate bracket.  I used to have an old expired Indiana plate (that I liked the design of) up there.  It was never questioned by any of the local constables (why would they question it - it can't talk  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ), even when I was stopped a few times for other reasons. <P>Reminds me - One time had to haul a Fiero rear bumper fascia from Fort Wayne, IN to Wichita.  Removed existing fascia from Fiero in Wichita, put plate in rear window directly behind driver's seat, then drove to Fort Wayne.  In Fort Wayne, installed fascia onto Fiero and drove back to Wichita.  Never harassed.  BTW, with the plate in back window directly behind my head, it didn't affect my view at all.  Worked like a charm!   <BR>||||reg||6
